rer's standings and the technical development cycle.\n\n       Tone Check:* Avoid \"disastrous,\" \"tragic,\" \"miraculous.\" Use \"inefficient,\" \"mathematical disadvantage,\" \"gap of X seconds.\"\n       Refining the \"Bagnaia\" problem:* The contact in FP1 (mentioned in Corpo 1) isn't just a delay; it's a disruption of the technical testing cycle.\n       Drafting (Mental or scratchpad):*\n        The 13th place position for Francesco Bagnaia is a technical setback that impacts the mathematical trajectory of his championship run. Motegi is a track where braking stability is everything. Being 13th means he is likely struggling with the transition between the heavy braking and the acceleration phases. The contact in FP1 didn't just cost 10 seconds; it prevented the team from calibrating the electronics and the traction control for the specific heat of the afternoon.\n        The Sprint Race, which offers 12 points for a win, becomes a high-risk operation. Starting from the third row means Bagnaia will have to fight through a high-density pack in the first two laps, where the probability of losing further positions increases by a factor related to the track width at Motegi. If he fails to break into the top 5 during the Sprint, the points deficit to his rivals will widen by at least 15-20 points by the end of the weekend, a gap that, statistically, is difficult to close in the final stages of the season.\n        Furthermore, the performance of Maverick Viñales suggests that the aerodynamic package used by Aprilia is optimized for the stop-and-go layout. For Ducati, this isn't just a sporting issue; it's a data gap. If the gap to the pole position remains above 0.3 seconds, the technical team will have to re-evaluate the engine mapping for the main Race.\n\n       Self-Correction during drafting:* The prompt asks for 350-400 words. I need to expand the analysis of the \"Sprint Race\" impact and the technical implications of the Motegi layout.\n\n       Drafting in